<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gomti River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gomati</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gomti is a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, rising near Pilibhit and flowing about 900 km south-east past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaunpur">Jaunpur</a> before joining the Ganges below <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>. In ancient times it bounded the kingdom of Kosala, and it is named in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> texts as a sacred river.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gomti-river.jpg" alt="Gomti River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original uploader was Faizhaider at en.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gomti_at_Lucknow.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
